In the `collect` method, `new BytesRef(docValues.nextValue().toString())` creates an intermediate `String` via `toString()` before constructing a new `BytesRef`. For keyword/flattened doc values, `nextValue()` typically already returns a `BytesRef`. This double conversion (BytesRef → String → BytesRef) is avoidable. Consider:
```java
Object v = docValues.nextValue();
values.add(v instanceof BytesRef br ? br : new BytesRef(v.toString()));
```
This avoids the unnecessary allocation and copy when the value is already a `BytesRef`.

